<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kris">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as keris, dhuwung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The kris or keris is an asymmetrical Javanese dagger whose blade is patterned by laminations of iron and nickelous iron, called pamor; many have a wavy blade, though older ones were mostly straight. Its development most probably took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, and a Chinese account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> describes men from the king to commoners carrying such daggers; the oldest known surviving kris, the Kris of Knaud, bears a date corresponding to 1342. Both weapon and spiritual object, it served as a talisman, an heirloom, part of ceremonial dress and an indicator of social status, and was forged by master smiths called empu. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed it as intangible heritage in 2005.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kris.jpg" alt="Kris" width="330" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Javaanse_kris,_AK-MAK-318.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
